1
00:00:00,110 --> 00:00:00,980
Hi guys.

2
00:00:01,280 --> 00:00:09,960
Let's talk about emails and learn what acronyms are and when to use them.

3
00:00:09,980 --> 00:00:11,480
Let's go.

4
00:00:12,530 --> 00:00:19,400
But before we start talking about emails, let's try to pronounce your email.

5
00:00:19,430 --> 00:00:22,580
My email is pronounced as

6
00:00:22,700 --> 00:00:28,250
kate@gmail.com.

7
00:00:28,880 --> 00:00:32,330
This sign is pronounced as at.

8
00:00:32,720 --> 00:00:35,810
And this dot.

9
00:00:36,050 --> 00:00:36,950
Great.

10
00:00:36,980 --> 00:00:41,360
Pause the video and try pronouncing your email.

11
00:00:42,680 --> 00:00:43,670
Good job.

12
00:00:43,790 --> 00:00:48,500
Okay, let's have a look at the structure of an email.

13
00:00:48,500 --> 00:00:56,880
So here we have a recipient, a person who is receiving the email here.

14
00:00:56,900 --> 00:01:06,200
Subject line is a short summary what the email is about Opening part.

15
00:01:06,440 --> 00:01:09,860
Hope this email finds you well.

16
00:01:10,130 --> 00:01:14,510
Here we have a body of our email.

17
00:01:14,540 --> 00:01:17,150
Body is the main part.

18
00:01:17,870 --> 00:01:20,930
Here we have a closing part.

19
00:01:21,050 --> 00:01:23,990
Thank you for your patience and understanding.

20
00:01:23,990 --> 00:01:31,580
We value our cooperation and this is the sender of this email.

21
00:01:31,610 --> 00:01:34,340
Team Project Manager.

22
00:01:34,640 --> 00:01:35,420
Good.

23
00:01:37,520 --> 00:01:41,660
Let's have a closer look at this email.

24
00:01:42,380 --> 00:01:50,380
So pause the video, read the email and decide what is the problem here?

25
00:01:50,390 --> 00:01:55,130
Why is Tim writing to Mr. Smith?

26
00:01:56,660 --> 00:01:57,560
All right.

27
00:01:57,560 --> 00:01:58,430
Yes.

28
00:01:58,460 --> 00:02:06,140
Team is writing to Mr. Smith because there were some problems with the project.

29
00:02:06,170 --> 00:02:09,260
That deadline was missed.

30
00:02:09,350 --> 00:02:18,260
So let's have a look at the phrases in the email so we couldn't meet the deadline.

31
00:02:18,680 --> 00:02:29,370
Our estimations were not accurate, but we are aware of the importance of fast delivery of this project.

32
00:02:29,390 --> 00:02:34,100
We value our cooperations sincerely.

33
00:02:34,490 --> 00:02:36,860
All right, let's.

34
00:02:37,740 --> 00:02:40,830
Repeat the phrases from the email.

35
00:02:41,280 --> 00:02:48,000
Meet the deadline Project Delivery Best quality.

36
00:02:48,750 --> 00:02:50,250
Cooperation.

37
00:02:51,100 --> 00:02:52,480
Estimations.

38
00:02:53,680 --> 00:02:54,640
Good job.

39
00:02:54,790 --> 00:02:55,480
All right.

40
00:02:55,510 --> 00:03:03,100
Have a look at these two messages and decide if they are the same or different.

41
00:03:04,330 --> 00:03:05,050
Okay.

42
00:03:05,080 --> 00:03:14,880
Yes, they basically mean the same, but they have completely different style and form message.

43
00:03:14,920 --> 00:03:17,740
A subject line answer.

44
00:03:18,470 --> 00:03:21,770
Sorry I couldn't attend Skype call with you.

45
00:03:21,770 --> 00:03:23,180
I was taking shower.

46
00:03:23,210 --> 00:03:25,730
Can you call me back now?

47
00:03:25,970 --> 00:03:31,280
Okay, so this message contains too much personal information.

48
00:03:31,280 --> 00:03:35,450
It's too direct, and it sounds a bit rude.

49
00:03:35,480 --> 00:03:41,300
You can write this message to your friend, but it's not appropriate at work.

50
00:03:42,380 --> 00:03:43,600
This message.

51
00:03:43,610 --> 00:03:44,830
Subject line.

52
00:03:44,840 --> 00:03:45,860
Apologies.

53
00:03:45,890 --> 00:03:47,020
Hi, Kate.

54
00:03:47,030 --> 00:03:51,470
I am so sorry I wasn't able to attend our video meeting.

55
00:03:51,500 --> 00:03:53,840
Something urgent came up.

56
00:03:53,870 --> 00:03:55,610
Could we please reschedule?

57
00:03:55,640 --> 00:03:58,640
I apologize for the inconvenience.

58
00:03:58,850 --> 00:04:02,780
This message is more formal, more professional.

59
00:04:02,780 --> 00:04:08,570
It has a good apology and it has a good suggestion to reschedule.

60
00:04:08,690 --> 00:04:13,550
This is more appropriate email to write to your coworker.

61
00:04:16,350 --> 00:04:18,600
Okay, more examples.

62
00:04:18,870 --> 00:04:23,310
We have a message subject line payment details.

63
00:04:23,760 --> 00:04:24,870
Hi, Kate.

64
00:04:24,900 --> 00:04:26,550
Hope you are doing well.

65
00:04:26,730 --> 00:04:30,450
Could you please send me the payment details for Alex P.

66
00:04:30,750 --> 00:04:34,440
Thanks and looking forward to hearing from you.

67
00:04:34,470 --> 00:04:37,280
We have a nice polite request.

68
00:04:37,290 --> 00:04:41,610
Could you please send and we have a nice closing part.

69
00:04:41,640 --> 00:04:45,240
Thanks and looking forward to hearing from you.

70
00:04:45,270 --> 00:04:46,440
This is nice.

71
00:04:46,440 --> 00:04:50,460
Appropriate example of a professional email.

72
00:04:52,710 --> 00:04:53,550
Okay.

73
00:04:53,640 --> 00:04:58,230
Sometimes you want to add some file or document to your email.

74
00:04:58,260 --> 00:05:01,590
Always mention that in the text.

75
00:05:01,620 --> 00:05:12,390
You can use phrases like Please find attached, please find enclosed or I attach a file about something.

76
00:05:12,420 --> 00:05:13,560
Okay.

77
00:05:14,860 --> 00:05:15,550
Good.

78
00:05:16,000 --> 00:05:21,040
Here we see a letter with just a subject line.

79
00:05:21,040 --> 00:05:24,130
Sent me payment details ASAP.

80
00:05:24,580 --> 00:05:32,950
Please never send a message without a body with just a subject line because it sounds a bit rude.

81
00:05:35,560 --> 00:05:36,280
Good.

82
00:05:36,460 --> 00:05:40,000
So people in it are very busy people.

83
00:05:40,000 --> 00:05:47,650
So sometimes they like to write very short messages and use acronyms.

84
00:05:47,650 --> 00:05:54,760
So let's have a look at this sentences and figure out what these acronyms mean.

85
00:05:55,030 --> 00:05:55,720
Okay.

86
00:05:55,750 --> 00:05:59,770
T h x for your help with the project.

87
00:05:59,770 --> 00:06:00,760
That's right.

88
00:06:00,790 --> 00:06:03,640
Thanks for your help with the project.

89
00:06:04,240 --> 00:06:07,810
I will be AFK for two hours.

90
00:06:08,050 --> 00:06:12,250
I will be away from keyboard for two hours.

91
00:06:12,250 --> 00:06:14,890
This means I will be offline.

92
00:06:15,640 --> 00:06:16,300
Okay.

93
00:06:16,300 --> 00:06:24,130
This is a comment on git and it's pronounced as l g t m.

94
00:06:24,430 --> 00:06:27,550
This means looks good to me.

95
00:06:27,580 --> 00:06:28,210
Approved.

96
00:06:28,240 --> 00:06:29,200
Looks good.

97
00:06:29,500 --> 00:06:32,740
All right, send me the details.

98
00:06:32,770 --> 00:06:37,130
P l z send me the details, please.

99
00:06:38,180 --> 00:06:41,630
Sorry, I cannot talk to you right now.

100
00:06:41,660 --> 00:06:43,310
I am.

101
00:06:43,880 --> 00:06:45,980
I am at the meeting.

102
00:06:47,480 --> 00:06:51,830
FYI, team is having a call with his lead.

103
00:06:51,920 --> 00:06:56,780
For your information, team is having a call with his lead.

104
00:06:57,770 --> 00:06:58,790
L m k.

105
00:06:58,820 --> 00:07:04,400
When you finish the task, let me know when you finish the task.

106
00:07:04,730 --> 00:07:05,960
Okay.

107
00:07:06,260 --> 00:07:07,140
Good job.

108
00:07:07,160 --> 00:07:08,410
Time for practice.

109
00:07:08,420 --> 00:07:09,290
Thank you.

110
00:07:09,290 --> 00:07:10,430
And bye.
